## Standalone Windows assistant and built-in skills

Direction: keep `gemma.ps1` self-contained and compact. Windows capabilities are exposed to the model through a small skill system; task-specific instructions are loaded on demand, while execution continues through the existing PowerShell tool and permission gate. Do not create a large mandatory helper-module tree.

### Implemented on branch `work`

- [x] Add an on-demand `get_skill` model tool. `get_skill("list")` returns available skills; requesting a named skill returns its task playbook.
- [x] Add built-in `file-management` skill: inspect first, plan file moves, detect collisions, avoid overwrite/deletion by default, verify results.
- [x] Add built-in `windows-settings` skill: use supported `ms-settings:` pages and supported cmdlets; distinguish opening a page from actually changing a setting.
- [x] Add built-in `desktop-gui` skill: launch apps, identify windows, use Win32 window APIs and semantic UI Automation, avoid uncertain coordinate clicks.
- [x] Add built-in `clipboard` skill: task-scoped Unicode clipboard read/write guidance, native Win32 API guidance, no polling, no startup reads, no clipboard logging.
- [x] Add built-in `system-inspection` skill: read-only CIM, process, service, storage, network and installed-app diagnostics.
- [x] Update the system instruction to load the relevant skill before Windows file/configuration/GUI/clipboard/system tasks.
- [x] Keep all actual command execution behind existing `run_powershell` and ASK/AUTOPILOT permission handling.
- [x] Preserve the protected API-key block exactly. No ACL, ownership, permission changes, or elevation code added.

Implementation commit: `9e95428bf7beb289be85527271ad0175f4af567f`. File blob verified as `329b4c55d6f0a4b40b5789143ef09b211041207a`.

### Implemented standalone CLI and Windows integration on `work`

#### User-facing command line

- [x] Replace batch-oriented flags with two short interactive options:
  - `gemma -p "show weather"` — one-shot interactive assistant request. Normal spinner, tool execution, and ASK confirmations remain active; final response is ordinary human-readable text.
  - `gemma -e "find notes.txt and open Explorer selecting it"` — one-shot command-generation mode. Return a concise Windows PowerShell command only; never execute it or call tools.
- [x] Keep no-argument `gemma` as the normal interactive chat.
- [x] Remove public `-Headless`, `-Prompt`, `-Json`, `-Stdin`, `-Search`, `-Command`, and `-CommandArgument` entry points. No machine-readable or redirected-input mode is part of the user-facing CLI.
- [x] Keep ASK as the normal permission mode. `-p` remains interactive and can ask the user before running a local PowerShell action.
- [x] In `-e` mode disable all model tools and Google grounding, explicitly instruct the model not to execute anything, and strip accidental Markdown code fences from the generated command.

#### Dedicated quick web mini-tool

- [x] Add a restricted `quick_web` tool, implemented with `curl.exe` when available (fallback: `Invoke-WebRequest`).
- [x] `weather`: fetch `https://wttr.in/?format=3`; no location argument means wttr.in chooses the region from the caller's IP. A user-specified city can be passed explicitly.
- [x] `public-ip`: fetch `https://api.ipify.org`.
- [x] `cheatsheet`: fetch `https://cht.sh/` for its home page, or a topic-specific `cht.sh` path.
- [x] Restrict the helper to these three fixed services, use request timeouts and response-size limits, and report curl/network failures.
- [x] Add the `quick-web-tools` skill and system guidance so Mira chooses this mini-tool for weather, public-IP and quick command-help requests instead of broad Google Search.

#### Native Windows integration

- [x] Add direct local commands: `system-info`, `process-list`, `clipboard-read`, `clipboard-write`, `window-list`, `window-activate`, `ui-tree`, `ui-action`, `app-launch`, `settings-open`, `network-check`, and `file-search`.
- [x] Implement lazy-loaded Win32 Unicode clipboard read/write and visible top-level window discovery/activation.
- [x] Implement UI Automation control-tree inspection and guarded `Invoke`, `Toggle`, and `SetValue` actions.
- [x] Add whitelist-based Windows Settings launching and application launching.
- [x] Add built-in skills: `file-management`, `windows-settings`, `desktop-gui`, `clipboard`, `system-inspection`, and `quick-web-tools`.
- [x] Add user-authored Markdown skills from `$HOME\.gemma\skills\`; validate skill names, limit files to 32 KB, and treat contents as untrusted guidance.
- [x] Add interactive `.skills` and `.skill <name>` commands.
- [x] Keep normal model-requested PowerShell execution behind ASK/AUTOPILOT. Clipboard writes and GUI actions also use the permission policy.

#### Usage examples

```powershell
# Ask Mira to use a mini-tool / Windows capability as needed
gemma -p 'show weather'
gemma -p 'show my ip'
gemma -p 'show help with curl cht.sh/'
gemma -p 'find my notes.txt and open Explorer selecting that file'

# Generate a command only; it is printed, never executed
gemma -e 'find notes.txt and open Explorer selecting that file'

# Normal interactive chat
gemma
```

